Enemy AI planning

Steps:
1. Gather context
2. Compute possible moves - compute values
3. Compute possible primary actions - compute values
4. Rank values against priorities / weights
5. Commit action.


Step 0: Gather context (cheap caches)

	Precompute per planning pass:
	Player unit list + positions
	Enemy unit list + positions
	For each player unit: its threat area (tiles it can attack from its current position, or move+attack if your player can do that)
	For each candidate move cell of the acting enemy: distance to nearest player, cover/terrain modifiers, etc.
	This lets you score risk/position quickly without recomputing everything for each candidate.

Step 1: Enumerate candidate move endpoints

Compute the enemy’s reachable cells (plus “stay put”).

Optional filters (huge perf win):
	Remove cells that are strictly dominated:
		same distance to all relevant players but worse terrain
	Limit to top N cells by a cheap heuristic:
		closest-to-best-target
		best defensive terrain
		nearest objective

Step 2: From each endpoint, enumerate candidate actions (These computed by threat range, not recomputed for each move tile)

For each move endpoint m:
	Attack candidates: for each enemy attack action, list valid targets from m
	Heal candidates: valid ally targets from m
	Disable/debuff candidates: valid targets from m
	Wait candidate: no action (or defend)
Represent each as a concrete candidate:
	Candidate = (moveEndpoint, actionType, target)
This is key: your scoring becomes consistent.

Step 3: Score each candidate with a structured scoring function

Split into two parts (helps avoid double-counting):

A) PositionScore(moveEndpoint)
	Things you can score without simulating an action:
		Safety / exposure: how many player units threaten this tile (or threaten after move if that’s part of player rules)
		Terrain benefit: cover/avoid/defense
		Formation / support: near allies (if you want)
		Goal pressure: proximity to objectives / choke points
B) ActionScore(action, target, fromCell)
	Immediate effect estimates:
	Damage / heal / status utility
	Kill bonus (very high)
	Disable bonus (scaled by target value)
	Counterattack / retaliation penalty
	Overkill waste penalty (small)
Then:
	TotalScore = PositionScore + ActionScore

Step 4: Pick best candidate (with tie-breakers)

Tie-breakers help AI look less random:
	prefer kills
	prefer less movement (more stable visuals)
	prefer higher safety if scores close
	small randomness within top few (optional)

Step 5: Build the plan from the winning candidate

Return:
	MoveTargetCell (maybe null)
	PrimaryAction + target (maybe null)
	Optional: “face direction”, “use ability variant”, etc.